# Accessibility Guidelines

## Default Expectations (when applicable)

- Proper ARIA labels (`aria-label`, `aria-labelledby`)
- Keyboard navigation support (especially for custom widgets; native elements like `<button>` already handle Enter/Space)
- Focus management (visible focus states; focus trap for modals/dialogs)
- Screen reader support (use `aria-live`/`aria-hidden` when needed, not by default)
- Semantic HTML (use `button`, `input`, not `div` with `onClick`)

## ARIA Attributes

### Common Patterns

```tsx
<button
  className="aceui-button"
  onClick={onClick}
  disabled={isDisabled}
  aria-label={ariaLabel}
  type="button"
>
  {children}
</button>
```

For native `<button>`, use the `disabled` attribute only; avoid redundant `aria-disabled`. Use `aria-disabled` for custom interactive elements (e.g. `role="button"` on non-button elements).

### Interactive Elements

- Use semantic HTML elements (`<button>`, `<input>`, `<a>`)
- Add `aria-label` for icon-only buttons
- Use `aria-labelledby` when label is in separate element
- Set `aria-disabled` for disabled **custom** interactive elements (not for native `disabled`)

### Form Elements

- Associate labels with inputs using `htmlFor` and `id`
- Use `aria-required` for required fields
- Provide `aria-describedby` for error messages
- Use `aria-invalid` for validation states

### Modal/Dialog

- Use `role="dialog"` or `role="alertdialog"`
- Implement focus trap
- Return focus to trigger element on close
- Use `aria-modal="true"`
- Add `aria-labelledby` pointing to title

### Lists and Navigation

- Use semantic list elements (`<ul>`, `<ol>`)
- For custom lists, use `role="list"` and `role="listitem"`
- Navigation should use `<nav>` element
- Use `aria-current` for current page/item

## Keyboard Navigation

- All interactive elements must be keyboard accessible
- Use `Tab` for navigation between elements
- Use `Enter` or `Space` to activate buttons
- Implement arrow keys for menus, dropdowns, and lists
- Use `Escape` to close modals/dropdowns
- Provide visible focus indicators

## Focus Management

- Ensure all interactive elements are focusable
- Provide visible focus states (outline or ring)
- Manage focus for modals (trap focus, return on close)
- Use `tabIndex` appropriately; use `tabIndex={-1}` only when needed for focus management (e.g. focus traps), not to remove focusability from interactive elements

## Screen Reader Support

- Use `aria-live` regions for dynamic content updates
- Use `aria-hidden="true"` for decorative elements
- Provide text alternatives for images (`alt` attribute)
- Use `aria-describedby` to link descriptions
- Ensure proper heading hierarchy (`h1` → `h2` → `h3`)

## Semantic HTML

- Use appropriate HTML elements for their purpose
- Use `<button>` for actions, not `<div>` with `onClick`
- Use `<nav>` for navigation
- Use `<main>` for main content
- Use `<header>`, `<footer>`, `<section>` appropriately

## Color and Contrast

- Ensure sufficient color contrast (WCAG AA minimum)
- Don't rely solely on color to convey information
- Provide additional indicators (icons, text, patterns)
